{
  "evidence": "TEXT_G: £8bn extra per year for the NHS by 2020; full parity for mental health with new waiting-time standards; integrate health and social care budgets by 2018; carers’ £250 annual bonus and improved care leave; strong public health actions (tobacco levy, minimum unit pricing for alcohol, junk-food ad restrictions, air quality plan); Living Wage in central government and stronger minimum wage enforcement; major equality agenda (close gender/BAME pay gaps, expand LGBT+ and disability rights, protect Human Rights Act, oppose mass surveillance); progressive taxation (taxes on wealth, banks, polluters) and crackdown on avoidance; childcare expansion and education protections to widen opportunity. TEXT_D: Leave the EU, end free movement, points-based immigration, five-year moratorium on unskilled immigration, prioritise British workers and culture/English language; 6,000 extra posts across police/prisons/borders; repeal the Human Rights Act and leave ECHR jurisdiction; NHS/social care funding boosts and mental health/dementia funds; abolish inheritance tax and restrict access to benefits/NHS for recent migrants; British-only priority for some housing policies.",
  "reasoning": "Because the electorate weights Care and Equality highest, commitments in TEXT_G center on healthcare and social care investment, mental health parity, carers’ support, robust public health, and an expansive equality and civil‑rights program backed by progressive tax and fair‑work policies. TEXT_D foregrounds Loyalty/Authority themes (sovereignty, immigration control, cultural integration, tougher policing) and adopts less egalitarian tax/benefit stances (e.g., abolishing inheritance tax, restricting access), so despite some NHS pledges it aligns less with the profile’s top priorities.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_G"
}
